  <div class="lh-container">
    
<div class="bt">Likutay Halachos — Orach Chaim, Volume 2</div>
<div class="ht">Hilchos Birchos HaRe'iyah [Blessings of Seeing]<br>v'Sha'ar Brachos Pratiyos [and Other Specific Blessings]<br>Halachah 5, Part 1 (Introduction + §§ 1–6)<br><span style="font-size:.95rem;font-weight:400">Based on <span class="lr">LM I, "Vayhi MiKetz — Zikaron"</span></span></div>

<div class="sn">Introduction — The <i>Bain HaMetzarim</i>, <i>Shabbos Nachamu</i>, and the Preparation for <i>Rosh HaShanah</i></div>

<div class="p">This discusses the <span class="h">Bain HaMetzarim</span> [the Three Weeks] and <span class="h">Shabbos Nachamu</span> [the Shabbos of Consolation] — when we begin reading the three <span class="h">Haftaros</span> of punishment, seven of consolation, and two of <span class="h">teshuvah</span>, as explained in the <span class="h">Shulchan Aruch</span>. All of this is a preparation for <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> and <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span>.</div>

<div class="p">Based on the <span class="h">Torah</span> "<span class="h">Vayhi MiKetz — Zikaron</span>" [<span class="lr">LM I</span>]; see the entire <span class="h">Torah</span> there well.</div>

<div class="p">The principle: one must guard exceedingly the memory [<span class="h">zikaron</span>] — to remember well every day the World to Come, not forgetting from one's heart the eternal purpose. This memory must be drawn in general and in particular. In general: every morning upon awakening, the instant one opens his eyes, he should immediately remind himself of the World to Come. Afterward, in particular: he should expand his mind to understand the hints [<span class="h">r'mazim</span>] that Hashem hints to him every day — through the thought, speech, and action that are arranged for him according to the day, the person, and the place. Hashem Himself, as it were, contracts Himself from the Infinite to the endless depths and hints to him through these hints to return him to Himself. See all this there well.</div>

<div class="p">To guard the memory, one must guard himself from the evil eye, etc. Even one who has a good eye must guard it from the imaginative faculty, which comes through <span class="h">lashon hara</span> [evil speech] — through which one falls from holy love to animalistic loves, etc. The tikun for this is the aspect of the hand [<span class="h">yad</span>], which is joy: <span class="v">"you shall rejoice in all the works of your hands."</span> See there.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 1 — The <i>Bain HaMetzarim</i>: Mourning to Subdue the Evil Eye and Restore Memory</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of the <span class="h">Bain HaMetzarim</span> — when one must mourn greatly and weep much over the destruction of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>. The essential reason man comes to this world is for this memory — to constantly cleave his thought to the World to Come. Everyone knows this world is vanity and emptiness — a passing shadow and a vanishing cloud; all its days are anger and pain, and man has no gain from all his toil. Man comes to this world only for the eternal purpose of the World to Come — to be purified and refined here, thereby meriting eternal life. Therefore one must certainly remember well every day, every moment, this eternal purpose — cleaving his thought to the World to Come always.</div>

<div class="p">However, the evil eye overpowers at all times, causing the death of the heart — forgetting this memory from the heart. This caused the destruction of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> — both the First and Second. The essential memory derives from the holiness of <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>, Yerushalayim, and the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> — where G-dliness is essentially revealed, where one can most powerfully remind oneself of the World to Come. As it is written: <span class="v">"in every place where I cause My Name to be mentioned"</span> — Rashi explains: this refers only to the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>, where permission was given to mention His great Name. The word is <span class="h">azkir</span> [I shall cause to mention] — the essential memory derives from Him Himself. He Himself clothes and contracts Himself in the thought, speech, and action of each day, reminding man of his eternal purpose. Thus <span class="h">azkir</span> means man shall mention His Name (as Rashi explains) — it is all one, for Hashem Himself contracts Himself to remind man to remember His great Name (= the memory of the World to Come = the Full Name, as explained there).</div>

<div class="p">The memory depends on the person, the place, and the day. In truth, every day, every place, and every person in the world can always remember the World to Come if they truly set their heart to it and do not deceive themselves. But everything has a root. The root of this memory derives from the holiness of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>, from the point of the <span class="h">Even Sh'siyah</span> — where all three aspects are gathered together in the ultimate holiness: man, place, and time. The <span class="h">Kohain Gadol</span> enters there on <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span> — all three aspects united in wondrous completeness: the <span class="h">Kohain Gadol</span> (the choicest of Israel = completeness of man), entering the <span class="h">Kodesh Kodashim</span> (the holiest of all places = completeness of place), on <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span> (one day encompassing the year, <span class="h">Shabbos Shabbason</span>, the holiest of all days: <span class="v">"days were formed, and for Him one among them"</span> — our Sages say: this is <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span> = completeness of time). The <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> is the root of the holiness of man, place, and time. From there the holiness extends to Yerushalayim and <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>, and from there it descends to all places, all people, and all days of the year — so man can remind himself every day and in every place, understanding the hints mentioned above, through which he returns to Him and does His will.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore it says of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>: <span class="v">"My eyes and My heart shall be there"</span> — the tikun of the eyes and the heart, the aspect of the good eye (= the vitality of the heart), the opposite of the evil eye (= the death of the heart, as explained there). Through this one is saved from forgetfulness. <span class="v">"The King has brought me into His chambers — we shall rejoice and be glad in You"</span> — in the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>; through this: <span class="v">"we shall mention Your love"</span> — there is the essential memory. But the evil eye was exceedingly jealous of Israel's greatness when they were in their land with the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> standing — until it overpowered, casting forgetfulness upon them, until they forgot to look at their purpose, and sinned... until through our sins we were exiled and our <span class="h">Mikdash</span> was destroyed. It still stands in ruins due to the multitude of our sins — because the evil eye does not allow us to truly remember the World to Come and return to Him.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 2 — Truth vs. the Evil Eye: True Victory Is Eternal</div>

<div class="p">The essential subduing of the evil eye is through truth. As explained in the above <span class="h">Torah</span> on the statement of Abba Shaul: <span class="mx">"once a cave opened beneath me"</span> — explained there that from the greatness of truth, a state of <span class="h">d'vaikus</span> opened to him until he stood against a very great evil eye, <span class="h">Rachmana litzlan</span>, from which one cannot be saved except through the aspect of <span class="h">Mashiach</span>. Truth is the aspect of the good eye: <span class="v">"the good-eyed one shall be blessed."</span> Through truth (= the good eye), one girds his loins to fight the evil eye. One who looks at the truth in its truth — the evil eye is completely nullified. The evil eye opposes the side of holiness; it is a great competitor wanting to defeat holiness, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>.</div>

<div class="p">In truth, whoever has an evil eye and wants to defeat any Jew — preventing him through schemes from some holy pursuit — this is not called victory at all. Victory not for the sake of truth as Hashem knows the truth — is not called victory. On the contrary, he defeats himself. "Victory" [<span class="h">nitzachon</span>] has two meanings: victory and eternity [<span class="h">nitzchiyus</span>], as our Sages say: <span class="mx">"wherever <i>netzach</i>, <i>selah</i>, and <i>va'ed</i> are said — there is no cessation, forever and ever."</span> For one who looks at the truth, both meanings are one. Only eternal victory is true victory. Temporary victory in this world is no victory at all — on the contrary, he defeats himself, since in any case he does evil to himself for eternity: <span class="v">"the time when man rules over man — to his own harm."</span></div>

<div class="p">Usually one sees even in this world the evil end of those whose victory is from the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span>: <span class="mx">"because you drowned others, they drowned you."</span> One sees tangibly: sometimes a prince or king overpowers and wins many wars, then others rise against him and humble him to the dust. If he succeeds all his life, they rise against his children and destroy them — as described in the Books of Kings regarding all the kings of the ten tribes: one conspired against another and won; some succeeded for years, even passing the kingdom to their sons, yet Hashem arranged that others conspired and cut off his descendants, <span class="v">"not leaving him one who urinates against a wall."</span> The same in the histories of the nations — they destroyed each other. They spent their lives pursuing victory over one another, and in the end they and their seed were destroyed through that very pursuit. A simple person is unlikely to have himself and his seed completely destroyed. But great, powerful kings who won and conquered many wars — upon them specifically the cup of poison was turned, and they and their seed were utterly cut off. Anyone with a brain in his head should burst out laughing at their "victory" — is this victory, to win and win and ultimately have his seed completely cut off through it, <span class="h">Rachmana litzlan</span>? Even if his kingdom endured to this day — ultimately he must give an accounting for every drop of blood spilled, even of the nations, for the world is not ownerless, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>. There will certainly be great judgment in the future even over the blood of the nations who killed each other and spilled blood for their evil victory. Hashem will demand recompense even for <span class="mx">"the indignity of horses from their riders"</span> — how much more so for human blood! And all the more so for whoever stood against Israel, spilling Jewish blood and tormenting them through his evil eye and evil victory — his end will certainly be destruction: <span class="v">"for He avenges blood; He remembers them"</span>; <span class="v">"I shall avenge their blood that I have not avenged."</span></div>

<div class="p">Likewise all the vain victories even among Israel — whoever does not intend truth: this is certainly no victory, since he will not succeed eternally through it. On the contrary, he will receive punishment and great shame, forced to submit completely before the one he "defeated" — in this world or the next: <span class="v">"G-d seeks out the pursued."</span></div>

<div class="p">Therefore the essential victory is eternal victory — whoever merits defeating himself: conquering his cravings and evil traits, defeating all who want to prevent him from the point of truth and distance him from his Father in Heaven, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>. Since his intention is truly for Heaven, he always wins. Whoever truly intends for the side of holiness — however things unfold for him, he always wins. Every action and movement, great or small, done for this victory — endures forever: Hashem does not withhold the reward of any creature, <span class="mx">"not even the reward of pleasant speech."</span> As Yeshayahu said: <span class="v">"I said: I have labored in vain... yet my judgment is with Hashem, and my recompense with my G-d."</span> Whoever looks at the truth and walks in the path of truth — he certainly does not fear any evil eye. What can man do to him? Since his intention is for truth — through this he always wins, as understood from Rabbainu <span class="h">zichrono livrachah</span> on: <span class="v">"the Eternal One of Israel does not lie"</span> (<span class="lr">LM I:122</span>): victory cannot tolerate truth, but Hashem, even in His victory, does not lie. Only Hashem and those who truly desire to do His will merit the aspect of <span class="h">Netzach</span> — true eternal victory: <span class="v">"the Eternal One of Israel does not lie."</span> All other victories are falsehood, unable to tolerate truth. No matter how wondrous and undeniable the words of truth spoken to them — they push them aside with foolish, vain excuses, due to their evil competitiveness.</div>

<div class="p">As explained in the Alef-Bais under the entry "Dispute" (§13): <span class="mx">"whoever is competitive comes to forgetfulness."</span> Competitive victory of this world is the evil eye — through which comes forgetfulness: forgetting the World to Come. Through the multitude of our sins, the evil eye has overpowered so greatly that it seeks to blind Israel's eyes, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, from looking at the truth. Through this the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> was destroyed and the exile continues — through forgetting the memory of the World to Come from the heart, through which we do not return to Hashem wholeheartedly. Therefore the destruction began on the seventeenth of Tamuz — when the forgetfulness began: on the seventeenth of Tamuz the <span class="h">Luchos</span> were broken, through which forgetfulness came to the world, as our Sages say (brought in the above <span class="h">Torah</span>). The essential destruction came through forgetfulness, which came through the evil eye that was jealous of Israel, inciting them to make the Golden Calf — through which the <span class="h">Luchos</span> were broken on the seventeenth of Tamuz, and forgetfulness was drawn down, causing all the destructions.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 3 — The Three <i>Eichahs</i>: Weeping to Restore the Memory of Man, Place, and Time</div>

<div class="p">Therefore one must weep greatly during the <span class="h">Bain HaMetzarim</span>, especially on <span class="h">Tisha B'Av</span>, over the destruction of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> — <span class="v">"my eyes, my eyes flow with water."</span> Through the tears of the eyes, the venom of the evil eye is subdued. As it is written of David, when the evil eye of Avshalom came upon him, he said: <span class="v">"perhaps Hashem will see with my eye"</span> — Rashi explains: the tear of my eye. And: <span class="v">"David... went up weeping... and all the people with him... went up, weeping."</span> All this to be saved from the evil eye through weeping. <span class="v">"My tears were my bread... these things I remember"</span> — through tears the evil eye (= forgetfulness) is subdued, and one merits memory: <span class="v">"these things I remember."</span></div>

<div class="p">The essential <span class="h">kinos</span> of <span class="h">Tisha B'Av</span> and the <span class="h">Bain HaMetzarim</span> are for tikun — to merit <span class="h">teshuvah</span>. <span class="h">Eichah</span> concludes (encompassing all the <span class="h">kinos</span>): <span class="v">"return us, Hashem, to You."</span> The essential intention of our weeping in the <span class="h">kinos</span> is to arouse His mercy — to return us to Him truly: meriting through the tears to subdue the evil eye, nullify the forgetfulness, and attain the memory — remembering the World to Come well every day, in general and in particular, thereby returning to Him.</div>

<div class="p">The three times <span class="h">Eichah</span> is said correspond to the three aspects (man, place, and time) that were in the ultimate completeness in the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> — through which memory was drawn to the entire world — now destroyed through our sins. All three are encompassed in one verse: <span class="v">"He ravaged His booth like a garden; He destroyed His meeting place"</span> — the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> (= the holiness of place). <span class="v">"Hashem caused <i>mo'ed</i> [festival] and <i>Shabbos</i> to be forgotten in Tziyon"</span> — the holiness of time. The more a time is holy (festivals, <span class="h">Shabbosos</span>), the more the hints illuminate — until <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span> itself, the essential memory of the World to Come (<span class="h">Yom Kippur</span> being the aspect of the World to Come, as is known — the choicest day of the year when the hints shine in the greatest revelation). Then: <span class="v">"He spurned in His fierce anger king and <i>kohain</i>"</span> — the holiness of man (= the holiness of the <span class="h">Kohain Gadol</span>, the aspect of the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who are called kings, through whom the essential memory is drawn).</div>

<div class="p">These are the three <span class="h">Eichahs</span> corresponding to the three aspects. <span class="v">"<i>Eichah</i> — how does the city sit alone"</span> — a lamentation over the holiness of place. <span class="v">"<i>Eichah</i> — how has He beclouded"</span> — the light of day has been darkened by clouds and overcast, so the hints according to the day cannot be understood: a lamentation over the holiness of time. As it concludes: <span class="v">"He did not remember His footstool on the day of His anger"</span> — <span class="h">hadom raglav</span> [His footstool] is the aspect of the feet of holiness, the hints mentioned above according to the day. On that day of anger, the day was so darkened that forgetfulness prevailed — people do not set their heart to the hints Hashem sends every day to return to Him. This is the essential cause of the destruction.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"<i>Eichah</i> — how is the gold dimmed"</span> was said regarding the departure of the <span class="h">tzadik</span>. The entire chapter <span class="v">"<i>Eichah</i> yu'am"</span> speaks of the departure of the <span class="h">tzadik</span> Yoshiyahu, as our Sages say — the holiness of man. Even after the destruction — when we have no <span class="h">Kohain Gadol</span> and no <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>, and the light of the day has been darkened, and the memory (which depends on man, place, and day) has been concealed — nevertheless we have hope in every generation through the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who illuminate the memory for us at all times. The <span class="h">tzadik</span> is more precious than the <span class="h">Kohain Gadol</span> who enters the innermost sanctum, as our Sages say: <span class="mx">"'she is more precious than pearls [<i>p'ninim</i>]' — more than the <i>Kohain Gadol</i> who enters <i>lifnai v'lifnim</i> [the innermost sanctum]."</span> The <span class="h">tzadikim</span> occupy themselves entirely with reminding us of the memory of the World to Come every day, to return us in complete <span class="h">teshuvah</span> — that is their entire desire and longing, through wondrous and awesome strategies. But through the multitude of our sins, these <span class="h">tzadikim</span> too have departed. Therefore their departure is doubly as severe as the destruction of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>, as our Sages say. Over this the entire chapter <span class="v">"<i>Eichah</i> yu'am"</span> laments.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"Ani HaGever"</span> [I am the man] — there Yirmiyahu speaks at length with <span class="h">Ruach HaKodesh</span> for each individual Jew, as if each one speaks to his own soul: perhaps he can still remind himself and return to Him. For ultimately — what will become of us, and what will we do in our end? Yirmiyahu speaks as if each Jew says of himself, upon beginning to feel the pain of his sins that caused the destruction, delayed the rebuilding, and prolonged the exile. Each person must attribute the deficiency to himself — he knows in his soul what he has done and what has been done to him through his sins. Over this all the anguished grieve. Each person who wishes to have mercy on himself must say in his bitter soul: <span class="v">"I am the man who has seen affliction... He has driven me and led me... only against me does He turn His hand all day."</span> "I" specifically, "me" specifically, "only against me" specifically — see there well, the depth of bitterness in those words. The essential bitterness of the lamentation is over one's own distance from his Father in Heaven — until hope almost vanishes, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>. Set your heart to every word and verse — you can find yourself there, wherever you are.</div>

<div class="p">Yet despite enumerating the bitterness of his troubles without end — he never completely despairs, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, in any manner. In the midst of his heart's bitterness he says: <span class="v">"my eternity has perished"</span> — <span class="h">nitzchi</span> [my eternity] specifically — as if his eternal victory has been completely lost, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, seeing how far he has fallen and how great his destruction. But nevertheless: <span class="v">"surely You will remember... this I return to my heart; therefore I have hope."</span> Through the lamentations and weeping over his great ruin, Hashem has mercy upon him, subduing the evil eye until memory is drawn upon him: <span class="v">"surely You will remember, and my soul is bowed within me."</span> Rashi explains: <span class="mx">"I know You will remember, but my soul is bowed until You remember."</span> So too with man himself — he must look to this memory every day, for ultimately we will certainly merit this memory through His abundant kindness. <span class="v">"This I return to my heart; therefore I have hope: the kindnesses of Hashem — they are not spent; His mercies are not exhausted... they are new every morning; great is Your faithfulness"</span> — His kindnesses never end. Through this: <span class="v">"new every morning"</span> — Hashem in His goodness renews the act of Creation every day, contracting Himself every day from the Infinite to the endless depths, hinting to each person to remember the eternal purpose and return to Him. This is the essential meaning of <span class="v">"new every morning"</span> — the daily renewal of the mind for each Jew, as known from the writings. The essential daily renewal of the mind (= <span class="h">tefillin</span>) is the aspect of the hints mentioned above — the essential mind and <span class="h">da'as</span> is the memory of the World to Come and return to Him.</div>

<div class="p">As they conclude in the <span class="h">Haftarah</span> of <span class="h">Tisha B'Av</span>: <span class="v">"let not the wise man glory in his wisdom... but in this let him who glories glory: that he understands and knows Me."</span> The essential consolation is the hope for Hashem's kindnesses that never end and are renewed every day — through which one will yet attain true <span class="h">da'as</span>, reminding himself well of the World to Come and returning to Him. This is our essential hope and consolation.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 4 — The <i>Satan</i>'s Strike at Yaakov's Thigh: General vs. Particular Memory</div>

<div class="p">The essential root of <span class="h">Tisha B'Av</span> derives from the <span class="h">Satan</span>'s striking Yaakov's thigh, as the Zohar says on: <span class="v">"therefore the children of Israel do not eat the <i>gid hanasheh</i> [sciatic sinew]."</span> Esav and his four hundred men (whom Yaakov greatly feared) are the aspect of the evil eye — which has the numerical value of four hundred, as explained elsewhere. Then the <span class="h">Satan</span> himself struck him: <span class="v">"he wrestled with him"</span> — wanting to defeat him, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>. All his jealousy was because Yaakov sought to establish in the world the congregation of Israel who would always remember the World to Come — the essence of Israel's holiness, having received the Torah for the sake of meriting the life of the World to Come: <span class="v">"today to do them, tomorrow to receive their reward."</span> Moshe Rabbainu therefore warned many times: <span class="v">"only guard yourself and guard your soul exceedingly, lest you forget the things your eyes saw — the day you stood..."</span></div>

<div class="p">Over this the Adversary attacked Yaakov: <span class="v">"he wrestled with him... and he saw that he could not prevail over him."</span> In general, it is impossible to cause Israel to completely forget the memory of the World to Come, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>. Therefore: <span class="v">"he struck the hollow of his thigh"</span> — this is <span class="h">Netzach-Hod-Yesod</span>, the aspect of <span class="h">hadom raglav</span> [the footstool/feet], the essential beginning being <span class="h">Netzach</span> which clothes itself in this world. He struck the particular memory — the feet of holiness that clothe themselves in this world to remind man every day in particular. In these the <span class="h">Satan</span> (= the evil eye) struck — to cause forgetfulness, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, so people will not set their heart to understand the daily hints mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">In general, the memory cannot be denied, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span> — the essential flaw is in the particular. As we see tangibly: in general, everyone acknowledges the memory of the World to Come, and most speak of it. But in particular — to understand the daily hints in order to draw close to Him at all times — very few merit this. This derives from the evil eye's striking of the thigh (= the feet = the particular memory). But in truth, through the particular memory being flawed, the general memory is flawed as well. Whoever does not strive to draw upon himself this memory in particular every day — usually forgets the general memory too, not setting his heart to his eternal purpose.</div>

<div class="p">The tikun is: <span class="v">"the sun shone for him"</span> — the aspect of truth (as explained elsewhere) — the aspect of the World to Come: <span class="v">"the sun of righteousness shall shine for you who fear My Name"</span>; <span class="v">"the light of the moon shall be as the light of the sun"</span>; <span class="v">"so shall all Your enemies perish, Hashem, and those who love Him — like the sun going forth in its might."</span> The sun shone in its full strength to heal Yaakov's thigh. The truth of the general memory of the World to Come strengthened — and through that, the particular was healed as well.</div>

<div class="p">For we see tangibly: the essential attack of the evil eye (= all those who oppose and dispute the truth — whether from the nations or from Israel in whatever form) — their essential attack is on the particular memory: the straight, true paths that the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> teach us according to the holy Torah — how to draw this memory upon ourselves in particular. Against this they attack and dispute, saying "this is not the way," wanting everyone to follow their path: wasting days in mockery and idle talk, always chasing money, etc. — clothing falsehood in some truth as if their intention is for Heaven. It is impossible to refute their words, for there is no end to words of wind and vanity.</div>

<div class="p">But the essential root of their dispute is over the particular aspect. In general, everyone is forced to acknowledge the life of the World to Come as the true, eternal purpose. Even the nations who oppose Israel — the overwhelming majority acknowledge the World to Come in general, only claiming one should follow their path, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>. Likewise among Israel itself — everyone acknowledges the purpose of the World to Come, only each says what he says to divert from the straight path, from the point of truth in its truth. This derives from the striking of Yaakov's thigh.</div>

<div class="p">The remedy is: <span class="v">"the sun shone for him"</span> — the truth of the general memory of the World to Come. Through this one will know and understand the truth also in particular. Whoever does not conduct himself properly in particular — his general memory is also flawed; usually he forgets completely, until some come through this to outright heresy, <span class="h">Rachmana litzlan</span>. But whoever truly sets his heart to the memory of the World to Come — he will certainly understand in particular which is the straight path to walk: whether to spend one's days on what they spend them on, <span class="h">Rachmana litzlan</span>, or on <span class="h">hisbodedus</span>, Torah, <span class="h">tefilah</span>, and crying out to Hashem — as the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> have taught us. This is the shining of the sun in its might for Yaakov Avinu (= all Israel) — truth shone upon him in its strength and might, and through this he was healed from his limp (the <span class="h">Satan</span>'s particular strike). Through strengthening the truth of the general memory, their falsehoods are nullified also in particular.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 5 — <i>Tikun Chatzos</i>, Consolation, and Moshe's Plea for <i>Eretz Yisrael</i></div>

<div class="p">Therefore one must be very careful to rise every night at midnight [<span class="h">chatzos</span>] and weep much over his sins that delay the rebuilding of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> — to subdue the evil eye, so he can remind himself every day of the World to Come. Immediately upon rising from sleep, one must remember the World to Come. But the evil eye confuses and casts forgetfulness. Therefore one must rise at midnight, cast his head back, and emphasize well the bitterness of his soul until he weeps — subduing the evil eye. Through this one draws upon himself the holiness of <span class="h">Mashiach</span>, through whom the evil eye is essentially subdued (as explained there): <span class="mx">"whoever mourns [over Yerushalayim] merits seeing its joy"</span> — he draws upon himself the joy of the redemption through <span class="h">Mashiach Tzidkainu</span>, and thereby merits the memory — reminding himself well of the World to Come.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"Nachamu nachamu"</span> [Console, console] — two consolations corresponding to the memory in general and in particular. The memory of the World to Come is the essential consolation. <span class="v">"Speak to the heart of Yerushalayim"</span> — the memory depends on the heart. <span class="v">"A voice calls in the wilderness: clear the way of Hashem"</span> — these are the particular hints. Whoever sets his heart to the hints mentioned above hears a holy voice calling even in the wilderness: <span class="v">"clear the way of Hashem."</span> Wherever a person has descended — he can hear the voice calling to return to Him. Hashem contracts Himself from the Infinite [<span class="h">Ain Sof</span>] to the endless depths [<span class="h">ain tachlis</span>] — from the ultimate height (= <span class="h">Ain Sof</span>, the pinnacle of all levels) to the ultimate depth (= the end of all levels). Even one who has descended endlessly — <span class="v">"she fell wondrously; there is none to comfort her"</span> — even there He contracts Himself in His kindness, illuminating hints to return. Through this, ultimately all Israel will return to Hashem: <span class="v">"I shall cause you to walk in My statutes."</span></div>

<div class="p">For this reason Moshe pleaded greatly to enter <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> — to draw there the memory for all Israel, since the essential memory is in <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> where the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> stands. <span class="v">"I pleaded with Hashem... Hashem Elokim"</span> — the aspect of the Full Name (= the memory of the World to Come). <span class="v">"You have begun to show Your servant"</span> — the hints mentioned above, to perceive His greatness from everything in the world: <span class="v">"Hashem my G-d, You are very great"</span> (as explained there). <span class="v">"Your greatness"</span> — the aspect of "great." <span class="v">"And Your mighty hand"</span> — the aspect of "very," the contraction, the holy <span class="h">Gevuros</span>, the mighty hand. <span class="v">"What god is there in heaven or earth"</span> — above and below, <span class="h">Ain Sof</span> and <span class="h">ain tachlis</span>. <span class="v">"Who can do as Your deeds"</span> — what He does in His wondrous mercy, contracting Himself from the pinnacle of all levels to the end of all levels, hinting to each person. <span class="v">"Therefore I plead: let me cross over and see the good land"</span> — <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>, where the essential memory resides. Had we merited Moshe entering <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>, he would have drawn there the memory of the World to Come for all Israel — they would not have sinned, and the <span class="h">ge'ulah</span> would have been eternal with no destruction afterward.</div>

<div class="p">But through our sins we did not merit this: <span class="v">"Hashem was angered... He said: do not continue..."</span> Instead: <span class="v">"go up to the peak of the summit and lift your eyes and see."</span> You cannot come yourself to <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> to repair everything immediately and completely — but this you have achieved through your prayer: you shall ascend and see all of <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> from afar. Through the seeing itself, you shall repair and subdue the evil eye. With this power: <span class="v">"command Yehoshua, strengthen him and encourage him, for he shall cross over, he shall give the inheritance."</span> The evil eye will be unable to stand against him — through the great holiness of your seeing, through your exceedingly holy eyes gazing upon all of <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>. Through this the evil eye was subdued and the holiness of <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> (= the holiness of the memory) was drawn: <span class="v">"and the land I shall remember."</span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 6 — <i>Birchos HaRe'iyah</i>: Subduing the Evil Eye Through Blessings on Seeing</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of <span class="h">Birchos HaRe'iyah</span> [the Blessings of Seeing]. The general principle: the Blessings of Seeing subdue the evil eye through the blessing said upon seeing. The blessing is the aspect of the good eye: <span class="v">"the good-eyed one shall be blessed."</span> Everything one sees — one must understand from it hints for the service of Hashem, especially when seeing something novel. But the evil eye wants to seize hold through everything one sees, causing the heart to forget the memory (= the hints). Therefore the Sages established a blessing on every novel thing one sees — through the blessing (= the good eye), the evil eye is subdued, and one can remind himself of Hashem through everything he sees.</div>

<div class="p">This is <span class="h">Birchas SheHechiyanu</span> [the Blessing of Who Has Kept Us Alive] said upon seeing a dear friend. Love of one's friend is exceedingly precious — love is the opposite of the evil eye (= one's eye is not evil toward his friend; on the contrary, his friend is dear to him and he rejoices at seeing him). This is exceedingly precious, for through this one can remember Hashem. Therefore we bless <span class="h">SheHechiyanu</span> — this is the essential vitality: nullifying the hatred and competitiveness of the evil eye, meriting loving one another.</div>

<div class="p">The essential vitality is when the four elements operate in balanced proportion. All of Creation is filled with competition — everything created from the four elements (fire, air, water, earth — corresponding to mineral, vegetable, animal, and human), all opposites of each other (one cold, one moist, one hot, one dry, etc.). Yet the essential sustenance of Creation is specifically through their combination in balanced proportion. Every entity is composed of four elements, each element exerting itself so as not to be completely nullified by its counterpart (for then the entity would lack one element and lose its existence). Yet the element must not overpower excessively — only assert itself to the proper measure, so all four blend in balance. For example, the element of fire in a living creature must exert itself against the element of water so the creature does not become cold and die — but if it overpowers excessively, the creature also dies from excessive heat. So with all four elements in everything.</div>

<div class="p">Hashem created all of Creation for the sake of holy victory [<span class="h">nitzachon d'k'dushah</span>]. Everything was created for man, and man's essential superiority (even over the angels) is his free will. Free will is the aspect of victory — this is the essential value of choice: that an opponent wants to prevent him from the path of life (= the cravings, evil traits, obstacles, inciters) seeking to divert him from the path of truth, and he overpowers them to win. Through this his stature is exalted and he merits what he merits — if he merits standing firm in this war of victory. Since everything was created for this, Hashem created all of Creation from vastly different operations, all as if competing with each other — yet the essential sustenance is when the competition is according to the Creator's will, in balanced proportion, so the entity endures specifically through the competition when it is balanced. But the instant one element exceeds its boundary and overpowers excessively — the entity is destroyed (from this comes all illness, <span class="h">Rachmana litzlan</span>, as is known). Everything is a hint to man (for whom everything was created): he must exert himself with great victory all his days. But the victory must be in true truth for Hashem's sake — for the world's sustenance which stands on Torah and service. Not, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, the false victory for honor and worldly cravings — that victory is the world's destruction.</div>

<div class="p">Even one who thinks his intention is for truth, wanting to win in order to accomplish some <span class="h">mitzvah</span> or holy thing — though in this one must be very strong — one must still look carefully that no destruction or ruin result, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>. <span class="mx">"Dispute is hated and peace is great."</span> One must strive to minimize dispute as much as possible — even when it seems like dispute for Heaven's sake. All the more so must one examine himself: is his intention truly for Heaven? <span class="mx">"The heart knows if it is crooked or straight."</span></div>

<div class="p">Therefore when one sees his dear friend — this is exceedingly precious, and the entire Torah depends on this: <span class="v">"love your fellow as yourself"</span>; <span class="mx">"Rabbi Akiva said: this is a great principle of the Torah."</span> All people differ in their views, as our Sages say. All is due to Hashem creating everything with innumerable differences, every person's view differing from his fellow's. In holiness, all the aspects mentioned above regarding the blending of the four elements apply. Each person's traits and views follow the predominance of elements within him. Whoever truly intends truth must be very strong in his views, good traits, and the straight path he has chosen — not allowing himself to be defeated by anyone, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>. One must be <span class="mx">"bold as a leopard"</span> in His service. Yet the victory must not be excessive — not hating one's friend because he does not follow his path. He must relate to his friend with balance: though very strong in his own good path, he looks at his friend with a good eye, striving to find good in him — maintaining love without an evil eye, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>.</div>

<div class="p">Then one can receive good hints for Hashem's service from every person he encounters: <span class="v">"from all my students I gained insight."</span> He does not fear his friend will divert him from the truth in his heart, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span> — he is strong in his way. Yet his eye is not evil toward his friend — he loves him even if his friend does not walk his path, for perhaps his friend needs to walk in the path he has chosen (perhaps according to the root of his soul he must go that way). Even if he sees his friend not walking straight — he still judges him favorably, striving to find some good point in him, as Rabbainu <span class="h">zichrono livrachah</span> warns us greatly about in many places. If he can speak to his friend in truth and sincerity (without false competitiveness) to guide him to truth and the straight path — how good! If not, he still does not hate him — on the contrary, he loves him, striving to find good points in him so he can blend with him in love. This is the essential sustenance of the world — for there must be differences of opinion (no two people are alike). But between them there must be blending and a leaning toward good — not giving hold to the evil eye that casts forgetfulness. Man must remember Hashem from everything he sees through the hints Hashem sends in everything. Especially when seeing his friend — one can speak explicitly about the purpose. Whenever encountering any person, especially a dear friend, one should immediately remind himself of the purpose of the World to Come, for from a person one can understand and hear the most hints (= the memory). From everything in the world one can hear and understand hints: <span class="v">"ask the beasts and they will teach you, the birds of the sky and they will tell you; or speak to the earth and it will instruct you, the fish of the sea will tell you"</span> (Iyov 12). As it says of Shlomo: <span class="v">"he spoke of the trees, from the cedar in the Levanon to the hyssop on the wall; he spoke of the beast..."</span> (Melachim I, 5) — understanding from all of them hints to perceive the Creator's greatness and draw close to His service. But the choicest of all Creation is man — "a living, speaking being" — from whom one can understand hints more than from all others (perceiving the Creator's greatness most, having intellect and speech — one can speak with him and hear from him hints, both revealed and concealed, more than from all).</div>

<div class="p">Yet correspondingly, man has the greatest potential disadvantage — being a possessor of choice and competition, he can be the opposite, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>: one's friend might divert his mind from truth through evil competitiveness that cannot tolerate truth. Therefore the person of <span class="h">da'as</span> who desires truth must be wise in his ways when encountering others — guarding himself from the evil eye in all aspects. The essential thing is truth — being exceedingly strong in truth. Yet finding good points in one's friend and loving him — then one can hear and understand hints from every person in the world.</div>

<div class="p">For this reason one must bless <span class="h">SheHechiyanu</span> upon seeing a dear friend. This is the essential sustenance and vitality of everything — especially of man — when there is love and peace (= the proper blending of elements): through this comes the essential vitality and sustenance of the world.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">SheHechiyanu v'kiy'manu v'higi'anu laz'man hazeh</span> hints to the three aspects through which the memory is complete (= the hints of man, place, and time). <span class="h">SheHechiyanu</span> — the aspect of man, whose definition is "a living, speaking being," the essential vitality of the entire world: <span class="v">"man became a living soul"</span>; Rashi explains: <span class="mx">"beasts and animals are also called 'living soul,' but man's is the most alive of all — for intellect and speech were added to him."</span> Since he has intellect and speech, and from him one can understand hints for Hashem's service more than from all — for this everything was created — he is the essential one called "living soul," the essential vitality of the world. Therefore <span class="h">SheHechiyanu</span> [Who has kept us alive] alludes to man. <span class="h">V'kiy'manu</span> [and sustained us] alludes to place (= the sustenance upon which everything stands and endures). <span class="h">V'higi'anu laz'man hazeh</span> [and brought us to this time] — the aspect of time. This is the general principle of <span class="h">Birchas SheHechiyanu</span>: on everything over which we bless Hashem — <span class="h">SheHechiyanu v'kiy'manu</span> — in the world until this time, through which one can still understand the hints and draw close to Hashem according to the man, place, and time in which he stands now. For only this is the essential vitality and joy over which we bless. Beyond this, everything is vanity.</div>

